WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.370
You do nothing illegal at home.

2
00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:07.780
So why do you close the bathroom door, why do you leave your phone face down on the table?

3
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:17.190
Everyone who says "I have nothing to hide" does the opposite dozens of times a day — and the real question is whether a person being watched is still the same person.

4
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.280
Picture a building.

5
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:35.730
At its centre stands a tower, and around the tower, rising in a ring, floor upon floor of cells set side by side.

6
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:42.740
Every cell has two windows: one looking outward, letting the light in; the other looking inward, toward the tower.

7
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:50.870
The light coming from outside fills the cell from end to end, and the body of the person inside falls toward the tower like a figure in a shadow play.

8
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:56.600
In the tower there are curtains, blinds, corridors laid out at broken angles.

9
00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.250
The man in the tower can see the man in the cell.

10
00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:04.380
When the man in the cell looks at the tower, all he sees is darkness.

11
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:12.030
The person who described this building was Jeremy Bentham, in letters he wrote in 1787 while staying with his brother in Russia.

12
00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:16.960
He had it in mind for prisons, factories, hospitals, schools.

13
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.250
He called it the Panopticon, that is, the place that sees everything.

14
00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:26.660
Bentham's invention was not really the architecture; the architecture was only the vehicle.

15
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:32.070
The invention was this: for surveillance to be effective, it does not have to happen.

16
00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.080
There does not have to be anyone in the tower.

17
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:41.770
It is enough that the person in the cell knows there could be someone in the tower and can never find out whether there is.

18
00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:46.700
Bentham described this as a new way of obtaining power of mind over mind.

19
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.870
The cost was low.

20
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:53.520
One watchman, hundreds of cells.

21
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.010
Sometimes no watchman at all.

22
00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:04.660
In 1975 Michel Foucault returned to this design in his book Discipline and Punish and turned it from a building into a formula.

23
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:08.630
Foucault's sentence was this: visibility is a trap.

24
00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:15.800
Because a person who is permanently visible eventually takes the mechanism that watches him inside himself.

25
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.770
He sits up straight even on the nights when there is nobody in the tower.

26
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:29.820
There is no chain, no whip, no punishment; there is the possibility of punishment, and that possibility works more cheaply and more continuously than punishment itself.

27
00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.670
The prisoner becomes his own guard.

28
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:39.360
Power no longer looks for a hand to apply it; the thing that does the work is the possibility of being watched.

29
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.850
Do not take this for an abstract French idea.

30
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:47.140
Think of the black plastic half-dome on the ceiling of the supermarket.

31
00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:55.590
Is there a camera inside it, and if there is, where is it pointing, is it working, who watches the recording, how long is it kept?

32
00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.800
You do not know.

33
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.810
It was designed so that you cannot know.

34
00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:08.940
If you have pulled your bag a little higher on your shoulder while walking under that dome, then even though you have stolen nothing, you are inside Bentham's building.

35
00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.110
Think of the speed-camera sign at the roadside.

36
00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:17.680
Your foot comes off the accelerator even on the days when there is no device behind the sign.

37
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:26.450
Think of the open-plan office: it was said that productivity rises when the walls come down; what came down was not the wall, but what was behind it.

38
00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:29.620
And now the real question.

39
00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.150
You have committed no crime.

40
00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:38.360
There is nothing stolen in your pocket, your speed is under the limit, there is nothing on your screen to hide.

41
00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.490
So why does the way you walk change under that dome?

42
00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:48.700
Why, when you notice someone looking at your phone over your shoulder, do you tilt the screen slightly away?

43
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.550
What you are doing is not shameful.

44
00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.280
It is not forbidden.

45
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.970
It is nothing to be embarrassed about.

46
00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:59.620
And yet being looked at changes the thing being done.

47
00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.430
If what changes is not a crime, then what is it?

48
00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:07.560
The sentence "I have nothing to hide" looks innocent.

49
00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.730
It even looks virtuous.

50
00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:14.700
It carries within it a claim to openness, to honesty.

51
00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.510
But this sentence is not an answer; it is a concession.

52
00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:23.120
What it concedes is this: privacy is the covering of wrongdoing.

53
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.770
A curtain is needed only if there is a fault behind it.

54
00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:35.860
Once you have made that concession, the argument is over; all that remains is to determine who is at fault, and you are not the one who makes that determination.

55
00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:40.310
Yet almost none of the curtains in your life cover a fault.

56
00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.320
You close the bathroom door.

57
00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.250
What you are doing is not a crime, everyone does it, it is biology.

58
00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:50.780
Still you close it.

59
00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:55.390
You put your letter in an envelope; there is no state secret inside it.

60
00:04:55.390 --> 00:05:00.000
You do not tell your colleague your salary; you did not earn it by theft.

61
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.970
You do not tell your boss what you tell your doctor; what you are telling is the same thing, and the only element that changes is who is listening.

62
00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:13.540
The philosopher James Rachels untied this knot in an article he wrote in 1975.

63
00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:19.110
Privacy is not the destruction of information; it is choosing where information flows.

64
00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:25.800
The only way we are able to have different relationships with different people is by giving them different amounts of ourselves.

65
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:33.450
The difference between your spouse and your colleague is not only that you work with one and live with the other; it is that one of them knows what the other does not.

66
00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:38.700
In a world where everyone knows everything, there is no separate thing left called friendship.

67
00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.590
Nor is there love.

68
00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:44.880
Because the meaning of sharing comes from the possibility of not sharing.

69
00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:50.130
When you lift the curtain, what you lose is not the thing you were concealing, but closeness.

70
00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:56.340
The criterion Helen Nissenbaum set out in 2010 gives this a name: contextual integrity.

71
00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:04.630
A piece of information is not in itself private or public; the violation arises when it passes from one context into another.

72
00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:14.520
The result of your blood test is medicine in the hands of your doctor, a price in the hands of the insurance company, a risk in the hands of your employer, gossip in the hands of your neighbour.

73
00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:17.850
The information is the same information.

74
00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:21.020
What leaks is not a secret, but a flow.

75
00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:25.630
This is why the "I have nothing to hide" argument asks the wrong question.

76
00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:31.200
The question is not whether the information is embarrassing; it is where it goes.

77
00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:41.250
The legal scholar Daniel Solove, in an article from 2007 that takes up precisely this argument, says the surveillance debate is being conducted with the wrong novel.

78
00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:46.820
Everyone thinks of Orwell, of an eye looking down from above, of the telescreen.

79
00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:51.750
But the true counterpart of contemporary surveillance is Kafka's novel The Trial.

80
00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.120
Josef K.

81
00:06:54.120 --> 00:07:04.970
is arrested one morning; he cannot learn what his crime is, cannot see his file, cannot find out who made the decision, cannot find the place where he might defend himself.

82
00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:08.460
Nobody shouts at him, nobody beats him.

83
00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.750
There is simply a file about him, and he has no access to it.

84
00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:20.080
This is today's problem: a file is accumulating about you that you cannot see, cannot correct, cannot object to.

85
00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:23.570
Every single piece of data inside it is innocent.

86
00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:30.740
Where you go, what you search for, who you talk to, what you buy, what time you wake up and what time you go to sleep.

87
00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:34.550
Taken one by one, none of it is a crime.

88
00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:39.800
What emerges when they are lined up side by side is more than what you know about yourself.

89
00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.650
Then there is the practical test of the argument.

90
00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:52.220
The journalist Glenn Greenwald describes a simple offer he makes to people who say "I have nothing to hide": in that case, send me the passwords to your email addresses,

91
00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:56.510
and I will go through your accounts and publish whatever I find interesting.

92
00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:59.360
Nobody has ever accepted this offer.

93
00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:10.690
Edward Snowden gathers the same contradiction into a single sentence: not caring about privacy because you have nothing to hide is like not caring about freedom of speech because you have nothing to say.

94
00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:15.140
A right protects you not because you use it, but because it exists.

95
00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:19.430
And the most fragile point of the argument stands at the very end.

96
00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:27.080
The sentence "if I am not doing anything wrong, I have nothing to fear" assumes that what counts as wrong stays fixed.

97
00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:30.250
But you are not the one who makes that definition.

98
00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:38.860
A belonging, an opinion, a friendship, a search history that is ordinary today may be read quite differently within another framework ten years from now.

99
00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:42.990
The record is permanent; the criterion is not.

100
00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:49.200
What you are relying on as you defend yourself is not your innocence, but the present mood of whoever is measuring you.

101
00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:59.570
This objection has a far older form, and the interesting thing is this: the tradition that put it forward is a tradition that draws an extremely sharp line between right and wrong.

102
00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:04.660
Even so, and perhaps for exactly that reason, it forbids searching.

103
00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:14.230
The twelfth verse of the Hujurat chapter of the Qur'an forbids three things one after another: most kinds of suspicion, tecessüs, and backbiting.

104
00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:21.400
The middle word, tecessüs, means roughly "to go after someone's hidden affairs, to search out their faults".

105
00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:25.690
Notice that what is forbidden is not seeing a fault, but looking for one.

106
00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:37.500
The twenty-seventh and twenty-eighth verses of the Nur chapter come right down to the door of everyday life: do not enter other people's houses without permission and without greeting them; if you find no one inside,

107
00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:42.910
do not enter until permission is given; if you are told to go back, then go back.

108
00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:53.600
In the hadith literature that grew up around these verses, even the etiquette of knocking at a door is set out in detail: you do not stand directly in front of the door, you stand to one side,

109
00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.890
so that when the door opens the eye does not fall straight into the house.

110
00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:03.940
There are reports that count looking into a house without permission as a violation as grave as entering it.

111
00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:07.110
The word itself is also worth looking at.

112
00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:12.680
In Turkish, when we say "mahrem", what usually comes to mind is shame.

113
00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:22.890
Yet the root of the word is the same as the root of the words "haram", "hürmet", "harem" and "ihram", and it describes an area that is protected by respect, made inviolable.

114
00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:28.940
The reason the area around the Kaaba is called the Haram is not that there is something shameful there.

115
00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:34.510
Mahrem is not an ugliness that must be concealed; it is a dignity with a fence around it.

116
00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:38.640
The curtain is not the covering of a shame, but the mark of a boundary.

117
00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:44.050
A report often quoted in the commentaries brings this distinction onto the stage.

118
00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:53.300
The caliph Umar, walking through the city at night, hears a sound from a house, climbs over the wall and goes inside, and finds the man in a forbidden state.

119
00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:56.790
"You think you have disobeyed God," he says.

120
00:10:56.790 --> 00:11:05.080
The man answers: "Commander of the Faithful, if I have disobeyed in one thing, you have disobeyed in three.

121
00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:08.570
God said do not search, and you searched.

122
00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:12.700
He said enter houses by their doors, and you climbed the wall.

123
00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:21.630
He said ask permission and give greeting, and you did not ask permission." Umar falls silent, leaves the man and goes out.

124
00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:28.800
The critical point of the report is not that the man is proved right; it is that the caliph's wrong is described as a wrong of method.

125
00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:30.690
There was an offence.

126
00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:35.300
The existence of the offence did not make the method of finding it legitimate.

127
00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:46.790
Al-Ghazali, in the Ihya, dealing with the question of commanding right and forbidding wrong, turns this boundary into a rule: intervention can be directed only at what is manifest, that is,

128
00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:49.800
at what has become visible of its own accord.

129
00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:58.090
To sniff, to listen, to climb a wall, to plant a spy in someone's house in order to learn what lies behind a closed door is not permissible.

130
00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:03.980
The same tradition has a positive face to this as well: setr, that is, covering over.

131
00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:10.830
The hadith saying that whoever covers the fault of a believer will have his own fault covered has produced a social reflex.

132
00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:14.480
Not spreading what you have seen has been counted a virtue.

133
00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:22.130
In short, there are two separate commands here, and both look in the same direction: do not search, and do not tell what you have found.

134
00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:27.220
This principle turned into stone and timber in the Ottoman city.

135
00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:36.150
The house plan closed to the street and opening onto a courtyard; the latticed windows of the bays that jut out over the street; the high walls dividing the gardens.

136
00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:46.360
The lattice is there not to cut the woman inside off from the world, but to establish a one-way line of sight: from inside, the street is visible; from the street, the inside is not.

137
00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:50.810
An asymmetry that is the exact opposite of the Panopticon.

138
00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:56.060
More striking still is that this became a matter for litigation in the registers of the kadis.

139
00:12:56.060 --> 00:13:05.790
A complaint is brought against a person who has opened a window looking onto a neighbour's courtyard or window; the rulings given most often involve closing the window, raising it,

140
00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:09.280
or placing a curtain or a lattice in between.

141
00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:15.170
That is, privacy is not a value to be prayed for, but a right that can be claimed in court.

142
00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:20.260
And the answer to the claim is, in the literal sense of the word, a curtain.

143
00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:26.630
So the answer to the "if you are doing nothing wrong, do not be afraid" argument comes into view.

144
00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:33.000
This tradition accepts that wrongdoing exists; it even describes it in the clearest terms.

145
00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.650
But it also counts the gaze itself as an act.

146
00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:41.900
The issue is not whether you are innocent; it is whether the one who looks has a limit.

147
00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:46.350
A limitless gaze ruins something even where it finds no crime.

148
00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:50.480
Now put the glass in your pocket where that latticed window used to be.

149
00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.850
Nobody climbed a wall.

150
00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:55.220
Nobody forced a door.

151
00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:57.110
You gave permission.

152
00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:07.800
While installing the application you ticked the box at the end of the text that appeared, you pressed the blue button that said "I accept", and in that moment you legally gave consent.

153
00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:10.170
So what did you read?

154
00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:21.820
Aleecia McDonald and Lorrie Cranor of Carnegie Mellon University calculated that if an internet user actually read the privacy texts they encounter in the course of a year, it would take about 76 working days.

155
00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:24.510
76 working days a year.

156
00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:33.120
Calling the approval given to a text that nobody reads, and that nobody is expected to read, consent is a polite lie the law tells itself.

157
00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:42.210
An experiment carried out in London in 2014 turned this into a bitter joke: a clause was added to the terms of a hotspot offering free wireless internet,

158
00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:46.660
stating that the user was handing over their first-born child for ever.

159
00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:50.310
Within a few hours 6 people signed it.

160
00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:53.800
In Turkey, Law No.

161
00:14:53.800 --> 00:15:01.770
6698 on the Protection of Personal Data came into force in 2016; similar regulations arrived in Europe.

162
00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:08.460
These texts bring real rights: the right to access your data, to correct it, to demand its deletion.

163
00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:17.070
But what changed everyday experience was most often this: a box that appears on every site, for which accepting is the shortest way of closing it.

164
00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:21.520
Consent turned less into a choice than into a turnstile.

165
00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:32.210
In the Cambridge Analytica affair that came to light in 2018, it was stated that the data of as many as 87 million users had been reached through a small personality-quiz application.

166
00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:37.460
The overwhelming majority of those 87 million people had never taken any quiz.

167
00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:41.110
It was enough that someone on their friend list had taken it.

168
00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:46.520
That was the strangest side of consent: somebody else had clicked on your behalf.

169
00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:52.730
The real injustice of this arrangement is not that data is collected, but that the direction runs one way.

170
00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:56.060
You are transparent, the system is opaque.

171
00:15:56.060 --> 00:16:04.030
You cannot find out what is accumulating about you, what score you have been labelled with, why you see this advertisement and not that loan.

172
00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:07.680
The inside of the tower is dark, the cell is lit.

173
00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:13.250
Bentham's drawing, four centuries later, has been built without anyone being forced.

174
00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:16.900
But does nothing really change for the person who does nothing?

175
00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:29.350
The legal scholar Jonathon Penney examined the view counts of Wikipedia articles related to terrorism after the surveillance revelations of 2013, and found a lasting drop in visits to those articles;

176
00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:31.720
the rate was about 20 percent.

177
00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:34.250
Nobody had been arrested.

178
00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:36.620
Nobody had been fined.

179
00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:39.470
No ban had been issued.

180
00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:42.640
People had simply stopped being curious.

181
00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:47.890
This is the measurable form of Foucault's sentence: the prisoner became his own guard.

182
00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:53.140
And what was lost was not one person's crime, but the space in which all of us learn.

183
00:16:53.140 --> 00:16:57.910
What is more, the issue is no longer even the recording of the past.

184
00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:09.880
In the order described by Shoshana Zuboff in her study The Age of Surveillance Capitalism, published in 2019, the value of data lies not in showing what you have done, but in predicting what you will do.

185
00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:21.690
Your clicks, what time you go to sleep at night, how long you spent on a message you wrote and did not send, how many seconds you watched a given video; these produce not an archive about you, but a model.

186
00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:24.060
That model is sold.

187
00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:29.790
The product is not the advertisement you watch; it is the forecast of your future behaviour.

188
00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:34.400
The man in the tower of the Panopticon is no longer watching you.

189
00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:36.450
He is calculating you.

190
00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:40.580
And here is where the real answer to the question of the curtain lies.

191
00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:51.270
Erving Goffman, in his work The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life, published in 1959, divides life in two like a stage: front stage and back stage.

192
00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:57.960
On the front stage we play a role; you are a teacher, a doctor, a father, a civil servant.

193
00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:07.050
Backstage we put the role down; the tie is loosened, the sentence is left half-finished, things are rehearsed, mistakes are made, things are given up.

194
00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:14.860
What Goffman is really saying is this: the back stage is not the place of dishonesty, it is the condition that makes the front stage possible.

195
00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:17.870
There is no performance without a rehearsal.

196
00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:22.960
For a person to be able to change, they need a room in which they are not seen.

197
00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:32.530
A place where they can get an idea wrong and then correct it, delete a half-finished sentence, give up tomorrow what they defend today, be curious without answering to anyone.

198
00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:38.420
A person whose every moment is recorded does not try anything new, because the cost of trying is permanent.

199
00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:45.430
A person whose every sentence is archived does not change their mind, because the change is read back to them as inconsistency.

200
00:18:45.430 --> 00:18:50.040
In such a life a person does not make mistakes, but neither do they ripen.

201
00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:54.330
The price of surveillance is not being caught; it is freezing.

202
00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:57.500
When evening comes, you draw the curtain.

203
00:18:57.500 --> 00:19:00.030
There is no crime inside.

204
00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:07.680
On most evenings there is not even a secret; there are the dishes, the television, tiredness, a half-finished conversation.

205
00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:11.650
That curtain is not a confession, and it is certainly not a lie.

206
00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:17.860
That curtain is the drawing of a boundary: this is mine, and whoever wants to look in here asks permission.

207
00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:22.950
And when that line is removed, what you lose will not be the things you were hiding.

208
00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:27.400
It will be the idea that a person with nothing to hide also has a room.

209
00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:31.370
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

210
00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:35.500
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
